# Cross-origin isolation for the web build. Recommended, and measured.
#
# expo-sqlite runs SQLite in the browser through wa-sqlite. Its worker channel
# prefers a SharedArrayBuffer, and browsers only expose SharedArrayBuffer to a
# CROSS-ORIGIN ISOLATED page, which needs both headers below.
#
# WHAT WAS ACTUALLY MEASURED (2026-08-21, Chrome, expo-sqlite 55.0.17):
# This file used to claim the database "throws on startup" without these
# headers. That was tested and is NOT true for this version. Served with no
# headers at all, the app still started, wrote an expense, and had it survive a
# reload and a new tab -- wa-sqlite fell back to a path that does not need
# SharedArrayBuffer. 8 of 10 runtime checks still passed; the two that failed
# were the isolation checks themselves.
#
# The headers are kept anyway, because the fallback is an implementation detail
# that can change with a version bump or differ in another browser, and losing
# it would mean losing someone's records. But do not treat their absence as a
# guaranteed outage -- verify with scripts/webtest/persistence.test.js instead
# of assuming, in either direction.
#
# This is the Netlify / Cloudflare Pages format and is copied into the export by
# `expo export`. Other hosts need the same two headers configured their own way.
# `npx serve` and `python -m http.server` do NOT send them; scripts/webtest/serve.js does.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
